    <article class="lava-guide">
      <div class="container">
        <section class="lava-guide-grid" aria-labelledby="lava-example-title">
          <div class="lava-prose">
            <p class="lava-eyebrow">Work through the order</p>
            <h2 id="lava-example-title">What the 10 × 10 foot example means</h2>
            <p>First measure the <em>clear stone area</em>. Subtract large planters, paths, and other spaces that will stay uncovered. Multiply 100 square feet by a 2-inch layer (2 ÷ 12 foot) to get 16.67 cubic feet. Divide by 27 to get 0.62 cubic yard. That volume stays the same whether the stone is red or black, but the bulk weight can change with particle size, moisture, and the supplier's stock.</p>
            <p>At the example 1,500 lb/cu yd, 0.62 cubic yard weighs about 926 lb, or 0.46 US short ton. Half-cubic-foot bags need whole-number rounding: 16.67 ÷ 0.5 = 33.33, so buy 34 before any chosen extra. The 34 bags contain 17 cubic feet in total. A supplier may sell bulk in half-yard or whole-yard steps, so ask about the smallest available order and delivery charge.</p>
            <div class="lava-formula" aria-label="Lava rock volume formulas">
              <p><strong>Cubic feet</strong> = area (sq ft) × depth (in) ÷ 12</p>
              <p><strong>Cubic yards</strong> = cubic feet ÷ 27</p>
              <p><strong>US short tons</strong> = order yards × supplier lb/yd³ ÷ 2,000</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figure class="lava-figure">
            <img src="/lava-rock-bed-depth.svg" width="880" height="390" loading="lazy" alt="Diagram of a 10 by 10 foot lava-rock bed with a 2 inch layer, equal to 16.67 cubic feet." />
            <figcaption>Measure the finished stone layer after preparing the bed. Size the layer by volume before converting it to weight or bags.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="lava-info" aria-labelledby="lava-material-title">
          <h2 id="lava-material-title">Choose landscape lava rock for the job</h2>
          <div class="lava-info-grid">
            <div>
              <h3>Scoria and product size</h3>
              <p>Landscape “lava rock” is usually vesicular volcanic stone, often sold as scoria. Its many voids make it lighter than dense river stone by volume. Check the actual red or black product's particle size: a coarse decorative bed has a different look and handling from fine cinders. The space between pieces is part of loose bulk volume, so a mineral's solid density cannot stand in for supplier bulk weight.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>Depth and uneven beds</h3>
              <p>Set the depth for the appearance and function you want, then check the largest pieces against it. A thin layer can reveal fabric or soil; a deeper one takes proportionally more material. For curves, divide the bed into smaller rectangles or measure its area directly. If the ground slopes or has low spots, estimate those separately and use the optional extra field only after inspecting the site.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>Plants and upkeep</h3>
              <p>Rock does not decompose like bark, but leaves, dust, and soil can collect in its gaps and support weeds. Remove debris periodically. Dark rock can warm soil in sun, and stone supplies no organic matter; match it to the plants and climate. A fabric layer can separate rock from soil in some beds, yet it is not a permanent weed cure and can complicate future planting.</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3>Fire features need approved media</h3>
              <p>This calculator sizes a <strong>landscape bed</strong>. It does not specify media for a gas burner. A landscape bag may be marked unsuitable for fire pits. If you are filling an appliance, use the maker's approved media, fill depth, and vent clearance. Wet media can pop; follow the appliance manual's drying and lighting directions and keep clear until it is safe. Never transfer the bed-depth recommendation into a burner pan.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lava-paths" aria-labelledby="lava-next-title">
          <h2 id="lava-next-title">Use the right next calculation</h2>
          <div class="lava-path-grid">
            <a href="/lava-rock-coverage-calculator"><strong>Already have a yard or a ton?</strong><span>Convert a known supply to square feet at your planned depth.</span></a>
            <a href="/lava-rock-bag-calculator"><strong>Buying retail bags?</strong><span>Use the label's volume and weight to count bags, estimate cost, and plan transport.</span></a>
            <a href="/rock-density-chart"><strong>Buying by weight?</strong><span>See why supplier bulk weight changes the ton estimate.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="lava-faq" aria-labelledby="lava-faq-title">
          <h2 id="lava-faq-title">Lava rock planning questions</h2>
          {faqItems.map(item => <div><h3>{item.q}</h3><p>{item.a}</p></div>)}
        </section>

        <section class="lava-sources" aria-labelledby="lava-sources-title">
          <h2 id="lava-sources-title">Product and field references</h2>
          <p>Bulk weights are examples from <a href="https://www.landscapecentre.com/products/bulk-materials/lava-rock">Landscape Centre (1,250 lb/yd³)</a>, <a href="https://www.palmercokingcoal.com/products/lava-rock">Palmer Coking Coal (1,300–1,500 lb/yd³)</a>, and <a href="https://www.sutherlandscape.com/products/lava-rock-black/">Sutherland's black lava (1,420 lb/yd³)</a>. <a href="https://www.homedepot.com/p/100427379">Vigoro's red landscape lava listing</a> gives 0.5 cu ft and 3 sq ft at 2 in, and excludes fire-pit use. <a href="https://yardandgarden.extension.iastate.edu/how-to/using-mulch-garden">Iowa State Extension</a> discusses rock mulch, soil warmth, and maintenance. For gas fire features, consult the <a href="https://cms.firegearoutdoors.com/documents/All-instructions/90214A-FPB-BS-Outdoor-Square-Pan-Flat-Disc-Fire-Pits-with-All-Weather-System-AWS-Installation-and-Operating-Instructions.pdf">specific appliance manufacturer's media and moisture instructions</a>.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </article>

    <TopicClusterNav
